  would give you [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, undefined)[39m — a signature change that tracks nothing. So I want to settle the
  design before writing code. Say the word if you'd rather I just widen the signature and stop.

  First question, the blocking one:

  [1mThe fork:[0m a [38;5;153muserId[39m has to come from somewhere, and the three sources differ in [3mwhen[0m the id exists relative to the
  login call — which decides whether [38;5;153muserId[39m can be a parameter at all.

  - [1mServer returns it[0m — the id is the [3mresult[0m of authenticating, so it can't be an input. [38;5;153mlogin[39m would return it instead,
    and tracking happens after the response. Buys you a real, trustworthy id; costs you the parameter you asked for.
  - [1mClient generates it[0m — a UUID minted in the browser before the call, so it genuinely is an input parameter. Buys you
    the signature you described and works with the stub as-is; but it identifies a [3mbrowser session[0m, not a person, so
    "who logged in" stays answered by [38;5;153musername[39m.
  - [1m[38;5;153musername[39m is the id[0m — no new value at all; tracking just uses what the form already has. Cheapest, and reversible
    later; rules out nothing.

  The first two are not cheap to swap afterward: they put the id on opposite sides of the call, so the call site, the
  return shape, and anything reading the tracking data all change with it. The third is cheap to change.

  My recommendation is [1mserver returns it[0m. "Track who logged in" means tying an event to a real account, and only the
  server knows that — a client-minted id can be forged or regenerated, so it can't answer the question you asked. It
  does mean the honest change is to [38;5;153mlogin[39m's return value and a tracking call after it, rather than a third parameter.
